Then he plays that song to the woman that he wants to marry.

00:19:48.280 --> 00:19:51.420
And then when they have babies, it becomes a lullaby.

00:19:53.380 --> 00:19:53.720
Try it.

00:19:55.770 --> 00:19:58.500
And are there any other friends that I don't remember?

00:19:59.220 --> 00:19:59.660
Well,

00:20:00.090 --> 00:20:00.850
There's Becky.

00:20:01.350 --> 00:20:01.770
Becky?

00:20:02.680 --> 00:20:06.580
Boy, Becky would be really upset if she knew you'd forgotten her.

00:20:06.840 --> 00:20:08.180
Oh, dear. Why is that?

00:20:09.000 --> 00:20:11.580
Well, because you're always there to help her, I guess.

00:20:12.760 --> 00:20:14.400
Like the time we were all doing a play.

00:20:14.950 --> 00:20:17.530
What's the matter, Becky?

00:20:19.310 --> 00:20:22.140
But he's helping me. I'm so scared.

00:20:24.100 --> 00:20:25.900
Oh, honey, acting's not so hard.

00:20:26.120 --> 00:20:27.780
What is it that you think you don't know?

00:20:28.330 --> 00:20:29.470
Just about everything.

00:20:30.350 --> 00:20:35.370
Oh, all right. Well, honey, let's talk about the scene we're going to do together, all right?

00:20:35.650 --> 00:20:40.320
Now, here we are at home, and we're preparing dinner for Dad, right?

00:20:40.640 --> 00:20:44.040
Okay, now, he's been gone for a long time, so it's a very special dinner.

00:20:44.240 --> 00:20:45.240
So what would you be doing?

00:20:47.110 --> 00:20:49.960
Guess I'd be setting the table like this.

00:20:50.020 --> 00:20:54.110
Perfect. Here, we'll use a special dish, and I'll get something out of the icebox, okay?

00:20:55.520 --> 00:20:58.100
Dad's coming home. Papa's coming home.

00:20:58.960 --> 00:20:59.960
Here, let's use this.

00:21:00.250 --> 00:21:01.110
Special napkins.

00:21:02.050 --> 00:21:04.440
See? That's perfect.

00:21:04.770 --> 00:21:08.050
When you pretend to be someone who lives a different life than you,

00:21:08.250 --> 00:21:09.030
that's acting.

00:21:10.160 --> 00:21:13.570
Then there's Kara. You must remember Kara.

00:21:14.220 --> 00:21:16.040
That name does sound familiar.

00:21:16.360 --> 00:21:17.260
Who is he?

00:21:17.960 --> 00:21:19.600
She. Kara is a girl.

00:21:20.820 --> 00:21:21.380
Let's see.

00:21:22.970 --> 00:21:26.090
She interviewed you once for a newspaper we were writing.

00:21:26.390 --> 00:21:26.690
Really?

00:21:27.050 --> 00:21:28.990
Oh, I wonder what I said.

00:21:30.480 --> 00:21:34.080
Readers might be interested in the history of Shining Time Station, Cara.

00:21:34.760 --> 00:21:38.320
Did you know that my grandmother, Gracie Jones,

00:21:38.800 --> 00:21:41.140
was the first station manager here?

00:21:41.340 --> 00:21:43.520
And boy, did she have stories to tell.

00:21:43.740 --> 00:21:44.540
Oh, let's see.

00:21:44.620 --> 00:21:45.340
My favorite.

00:21:45.540 --> 00:21:48.290
What wonderful memories I must have had.

00:21:48.450 --> 00:21:49.350
I mean, had.

00:21:49.470 --> 00:21:52.090
I mean, will have very, very soon.

00:21:53.100 --> 00:21:55.300
Very soon, Mr. Conductor said.

00:21:55.460 --> 00:21:57.820
Now, Dan, tell me about this person's schemer.

00:21:57.940 --> 00:21:59.980
Does he wear a dark suit and a white hat?

00:22:00.220 --> 00:22:01.900
and red sunglasses, and a funny mustache?

00:22:02.500 --> 00:22:04.950
No, that is the way he dresses

00:22:04.950 --> 00:22:06.890
when he's pretending to be his own lawyer.

00:22:08.610 --> 00:22:10.770
Sounds familiar, is this his?

00:22:10.990 --> 00:22:11.970
Yeah. What is it?

00:22:12.230 --> 00:22:14.150
It's a jukebox, you put in a nickel,

00:22:14.750 --> 00:22:18.220
pick a song, and it plays the song you picked, but...

00:22:18.400 --> 00:22:18.780
But what?

00:22:19.490 --> 00:22:21.390
Well, sometimes it plays on its own,

00:22:21.930 --> 00:22:23.270
or it doesn't play the song you picked.

00:22:23.450 --> 00:22:24.110
Do you have a nickel?

00:22:25.910 --> 00:22:26.310
Yeah.

00:22:26.740 --> 00:22:28.500
Okay, let's put it in and see what happens.

00:22:36.110 --> 00:22:38.350
We've got to play something to help Stacey.

00:22:39.120 --> 00:22:39.720
Any ideas?

00:22:40.520 --> 00:22:42.360
Well, how about something country,

00:22:42.520 --> 00:22:46.380
like, I forgot more than you'll ever know about her.

00:22:46.850 --> 00:22:50.260
Or, I forgot to remember to forget.

00:22:51.190 --> 00:22:55.730
I heard it might help if Stacey just got some sleep.

00:22:56.170 --> 00:22:59.970
Dee Dee, baby, you are not only beautiful, you are brilliant.

00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:02.260
Let's play a lullaby.

00:23:03.640 --> 00:23:05.220
Yeah, something pretty.

00:23:06.200 --> 00:23:08.020
We'll play a couple of them.

00:23:08.720 --> 00:23:10.920
And a one, and a two, and...

00:23:30.670 --> 00:23:30.950
Oh.

00:24:30.380 --> 00:24:33.620
When you were killed

00:26:00.000 --> 00:26:03.150
Mr. Conductor, gee, was I sleeping long?

00:26:04.320 --> 00:26:05.720
Look what time it is.

00:26:05.880 --> 00:26:08.840
The rocking horse special is going to be here any minute.

00:26:09.120 --> 00:26:15.470
You know, I was having the funniest dream right now.

00:26:15.550 --> 00:26:16.970
Well, it was kind of a dream.

00:26:17.090 --> 00:26:19.810
It was a dream about a dream about memory.

00:26:20.130 --> 00:26:23.690
You know how important it is to remember who you are

00:26:23.690 --> 00:26:26.270
and who your friends are and what your dreams are.

00:26:26.640 --> 00:26:28.620
Does any of this make any sense?

00:26:29.170 --> 00:26:29.610
Absolutely.

00:26:30.440 --> 00:26:31.180
Positively.

00:26:31.900 --> 00:26:32.840
My key, sir.

00:26:34.500 --> 00:26:34.800
Uh-oh.

00:26:35.380 --> 00:26:41.730
There you are.

00:26:41.930 --> 00:26:44.130
My client, Mr. Schemer,

00:26:44.520 --> 00:26:47.560
is hoping that you could resign this as a...

00:26:47.560 --> 00:26:50.340
Yes, your client, ma'am.

00:26:50.530 --> 00:26:52.550
Since when do you call me ma'am?

00:26:54.080 --> 00:26:58.380
Oh, well, it's just a little joke, Jonesy.

00:26:58.520 --> 00:26:59.660
Ma'am, Mr. Jones?

00:27:00.190 --> 00:27:03.970
Yeah, and it's also important to remember who your friends aren't.

00:27:04.460 --> 00:27:11.600
Who would take advantage of someone for their own low, mean-spirited personal gains?

00:27:13.880 --> 00:27:14.140
Ouch.

00:27:24.000 --> 00:27:25.880
Take it that means you are declining my client's offer?

00:27:32.580 --> 00:27:58.260
Reach for the speed. Reach for the whistle. Go where the rail may run. Reach for the work. Reach for the story. Follow the rainbow sun. To a shining time station where dreams can come.

00:28:00.000 --> 00:28:09.890
So far to travel, so much to learn to know

00:28:09.890 --> 00:28:14.990
Friends by your side, hopes to hold on to

00:28:15.330 --> 00:28:17.330
Who knows how far you'll go

00:28:17.330 --> 00:28:21.930
To a shining time station

00:28:21.930 --> 00:28:27.800
Where dreams can come through your ocean

00:28:34.760 --> 00:28:38.500
Funding for this program was provided in part by the Corporation for Public Broadcasting

00:28:38.500 --> 00:28:40.840
and by the financial support of viewers like you.
